
  - [1mDevice-local ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys you a working feature with zero backend; costs you that preferences don't follow
    a user to another browser or device, and anyone sharing the device shares the settings. Cheap to build, and
    migrating to server-backed later means writing a sync/merge story you don't have now.
  - [1mAccount-scoped (server-backed)[0m — buys you real per-user persistence across devices; costs you a backend that does
    not exist here ([38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub returning [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m with no session or token). This is the expensive one,
    and it is not cheap to retrofit onto a fake login.
  - [1mDevice-local, keyed by username[0m — a middle option: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with the logged-in username in the key. Buys
    per-user separation on a shared device without a backend; costs nothing extra, but it is still per-device and offers
    no security boundary (any user can read another's prefs).

  My recommendation is [1mdevice-local [38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m, plain and un-keyed, because the login is a stub — there is no
  authenticated identity to scope anything to, so username-keying would imply a guarantee the code can't make. If a real
  backend arrives, preferences sync becomes its own piece of work.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should preferences persist? → Device-local localStorage (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Device-local [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m it is.

  Next: [1mwhat actually gets stored.[0m The app has no settings today — no theme toggle, no remembered username, nothing. So
  "preferences storage" is currently a container with nothing to put in it. What goes in shapes the API: a handful of
  known keys with defaults is a different module than an open-ended bag.

